cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  Cite the commit you read, by its hash and date, and the repository URL.
  The documentation is licensed under CC BY 4.0 and the code under MIT;
  the datasets keep their own licenses (data/SOURCES.md).
title: "Stochology: The Theory and Ethics of Aim under Uncertainty"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Neto
    given-names: Silvano
    email: dev@silvanoneto.com
repository-code: https://github.com/0b3st4f3r4/stochology
url: https://stochology.org
license:
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - MIT
abstract: >-
  A pure-Rust (std only, zero external dependencies) library, command-line verification suite,
  five foundational essays (notes/), and 86-chapter (00-85) five-part textbook implementing
  Stochology — an integrative framework and transversal research program for the theory and
  ethics of aim (stókhos) for judgment and decision-making under structural uncertainty when
  action must precede the outcome, whose distinct unit of analysis is the full mediation circuit
  across World, Archive, and Institution. Organized 1-to-1 around the Sequential
  Agent-World-Institution Loop (CommunityReserveEnv), the Leontief Nuclear Triad
  M_Leontief = min(S_epi, S_vital, S_gov), the Three Canonical Operators (EpistemicMarginState,
  VitalMarginState, GovernanceState), the Three Collapse Propositions, the Seven Guard Sites
  (T1-T7, 3 direct transits + 3 reflexive locks + 1 integral three-plane closure = 7 circuit
  transits in grammar.rs), the 35-layer Multilayer Knowledge Graph (G_1 <-> G_2, 7 strata x 5
  orders = C(7,3) = 35 in multilayer.rs), and 14 cryptographically sealed empirical datasets
  (data/SHA256SUMS).
keywords:
  - stochology
  - decision under uncertainty
  - epistemic margin
  - partial identification
  - vital margin
  - non-ergodicity
  - institutional governance
  - performative prediction
  - causal invariance
  - formal grammar of aim
  - multilayer knowledge graph
  - calibration
  - reproducibility
